# 5. PLAYABLE MECH ROSTER

Do not limit the player to a single mech.

Create a unit selection screen where the player can inspect, select, and test different combat machines.

Every unit must have distinct:
- Movement physics.
- Acceleration.
- Maximum speed.
- Turning speed.
- Weight.
- Armor.
- Weapon configuration.
- Reload speed.
- Weapon recoil.
- Leg arrangement.
- Leg animation.
- Special ability.

The units should feel genuinely different when controlled.

Create at least seven playable units.

### UNIT 01: RECON SCOUT

Role: Reconnaissance and rapid movement.

Characteristics:
- Extremely lightweight.
- Very high acceleration.
- High maximum speed.
- Excellent turning ability.
- Weak armor.
- Low weapon damage.

Equipment:
- Light autocannon.
- Basic targeting system.
- Enemy detection equipment.

Special ability: Temporary movement boost that increases acceleration and maximum speed.

Leg configuration: Four lightweight articulated legs.

The scout should feel extremely responsive and agile.

### UNIT 02: STANDARD COMBAT WALKER

Role: Balanced frontline combat.

Characteristics:
- Average weight.
- Balanced acceleration.
- Moderate armor.
- Good turning speed.
- Reliable weapon performance.

Equipment:
- Automatic cannon.
- Secondary weapon.
- Standard targeting equipment.

Special ability: Temporary defensive stabilization that reduces incoming damage.

Leg configuration: Six articulated legs.

This should be the default unit and the easiest unit for beginners to control.

### UNIT 03: LONG-RANGE SNIPER

Role: Precision engagement.

Characteristics:
- Low mobility while aiming.
- Moderate movement speed.
- Strong long-range capabilities.
- Low armor.
- Powerful single-shot weapon.

Equipment:
- High-powered railgun.
- Long-range optical system.
- Limited secondary ammunition.

Special ability: Enter a stationary firing stance for improved accuracy and reduced recoil.

Leg configuration: Four reinforced legs with wider support positioning.

When entering firing mode, the legs should spread out and stabilize the chassis.

### UNIT 04: HEAVY ASSAULT WALKER

Role: Frontline heavy combat.

Characteristics:
- High mass.
- Slow acceleration.
- Lower turning speed.
- High armor.
- Strong weapon resistance.

Equipment:
- Heavy autocannon.
- Heavy machine gun.
- Additional armor plating.

Special ability: Deploy reinforced armor that temporarily reduces incoming damage.

Leg configuration: Eight heavy mechanical legs.

Its movements should look powerful and heavy. It should visibly struggle when rapidly changing direction.

### UNIT 05: ARTILLERY WALKER

Role: Long-range indirect fire.

Characteristics:
- Very high weapon damage.
- Slow movement.
- Heavy recoil.
- Moderate armor.
- Low close-range effectiveness.

Equipment:
- Long-range artillery cannon.
- Explosive ammunition.
- Targeting system.

Special ability: Fire a high-damage artillery round at a marked location.

Artillery should use projectile physics, including travel time and a visible trajectory.

Leg configuration: Six reinforced support legs.

The legs should brace and spread apart when firing the main weapon.

### UNIT 06: ELECTRONIC WARFARE WALKER

Role: Reconnaissance, disruption, and support.

Characteristics:
- Average movement speed.
- Moderate armor.
- Specialized equipment.
- Limited direct combat damage.

Equipment:
- Disruption equipment.
- Detection radar.
- Light defensive weapon.

Special ability: Temporarily interfere with nearby enemy targeting and reveal hidden enemy locations.

Leg configuration: Six lightweight legs with compact equipment mounts.

### UNIT 07: ENGINEER SUPPORT WALKER

Role: Repair and battlefield assistance.

Characteristics:
- Moderate speed.
- Low offensive damage.
- Average armor.
- Strong support capabilities.

Equipment:
- Light defensive weapon.
- Repair system.
- Deployable support equipment.

Special ability: Repair nearby allied units over time.

Leg configuration: Six articulated legs with visible mechanical support equipment.

