# 7. WEAPON AND COMBAT SYSTEM

Combat should feel fast, dangerous, and mechanical.

Implement multiple weapon types.

### Automatic cannons

- Continuous firing.
- Individual projectiles.
- Muzzle flashes.
- Recoil.
- Bullet impacts.
- Reloading or overheating where appropriate.

### Machine guns

- High fire rate.
- Lower individual damage.
- Visible projectile trails.
- Weapon spread.
- Recoil accumulation.

### Railguns

- Powerful single shots.
- Charge-up animation.
- Bright projectile effect.
- Large recoil.
- High penetration.
- Long reload time.

### Artillery

- Ballistic projectile movement.
- Visible projectile arc.
- Explosive impact.
- Area damage.
- Large explosion particles.
- Strong camera shake.

### Missiles

- Lock-on targeting.
- Warning indicators.
- Homing movement.
- Limited turning ability.
- Interception or countermeasures.

### Weapon physics

Weapons should interact with the mech.

For example:
- Firing powerful weapons should push the chassis backward slightly.
- Heavy weapons should create noticeable recoil.
- Rapid firing should make the weapon harder to stabilize.
- Certain weapons should require the mech to enter a stable firing stance.
- Explosions should affect nearby units and environmental objects.

### Damage system

Do not make every mech have one simple health bar.

Implement individual component damage.

Possible components:
- Main chassis.
- Left legs.
- Right legs.
- Weapon systems.
- Sensors.
- Engine.
- Armor plating.

Damage to individual legs should affect movement.

Damage to the engine should reduce acceleration.

Damage to sensors should reduce targeting accuracy and detection range.

Damage to weapons should affect firing performance.

A heavily damaged mech should visibly behave differently from a fully functional one.

